{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "03-pncounter.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "The PN-Counter",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Add decrements to the counter while every update still moves the state up.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The like button needs an unlike. The obvious decrement: a replica lowers its own slot.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Replica $a$ has counted 2 taps, state $(2, 0)$, and $b$ has heard of them, so $b$ holds $(2, 0)$ too. Now $a$ decrements, to $(1, 0)$, and then $b$'s state arrives. Predict the merge.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let C4 = chain(4)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let G = product(C4, C4)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(G, (1, 0), (2, 0))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The decrement is undone. The join only goes up, the decrement went down, and $b$'s stale state, still above, wins: lesson 1's warning about updates that are not inflationary, now as a bug.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Count the decrements",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The way out is the move every design in this course makes: if a merge loses information, put that information in the state. A decrement is not the removal of an increment but an event of its own, and events can be counted, in a G-Counter of their own. Keep two G-Counters, $P$ for increments and $N$ for decrements, and read the value as $P - N$. Every update raises one of them, so the state only goes up while the value may go down.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] PN-Counter\n> A **PN-Counter** is a pair of G-Counters $(P, N)$. Replica $i$ increments by raising its entry of $P$ and decrements by raising its entry of $N$; merge is the join of each half; the value is $\\sum P - \\sum N$.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "For one replica a state is a pair $(p, n)$, ordered componentwise:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let C3 = chain(3)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let PN = product(C3, C3)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "le(PN, (1, 0), (1, 1))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$(1, 0) \\sqsubseteq (1, 1)$: a decrement moved the state up while the value went from 1 to 0.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The decrement survives",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The schedule that undid the decrement, on a PN-Counter: $a$ counts two, $b$ hears of them, $a$ decrements, and $b$'s stale state reaches $a$. The state is a pair of G-Counters, increments and decrements:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "replicas(pncounter; a, b\n  a: inc\n  a: inc\n  a -> b\n  a: dec\n  b -> a\n  a -> b\n)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "$b$'s state is no longer above $a$'s: $a$ has a decrement that $b$ lacks, and the merge keeps it. Both read 1.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!mistake]\n> The value of a merge is not the larger value. An increment at $a$ (value 1) and a decrement at $b$ (value $-1$) merge to 0, not $\\max(1, -1) = 1$:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "replicas(pncounter; a, b\n  a: inc\n  b: dec\n  a -> b\n  b -> a\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Only the state is merged; the value is read off it, and it is not monotone in the state.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The chapter proves both halves of that: `decr_inflationary` (the state goes up) and `value_not_monotone` (the value need not), with `value_merge_ne_max` for the mistake above.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/- ============================================================\n   Crdt.PNCounter — Chapter 4: Decrements and the\n   Query/State Split\n   ============================================================ -/\n\n/-- Two G-Counters: increments land in `P` (first), decrements in `N`\n    (second). State only ever grows; the *query* subtracts. -/\nabbrev PNCounter (R : Nat) := GCounter R × GCounter R\n\nnamespace PNCounter\n\nopen Order.PartialOrder Order.BoundedJoinSemilattice\n\nvariable {R : Nat}\n\ndef incr (i : Fin R) (p : PNCounter R) : PNCounter R :=\n  (GCounter.increment i p.1, p.2)\n\ndef decr (i : Fin R) (p : PNCounter R) : PNCounter R :=\n  (p.1, GCounter.increment i p.2)\n\ndef value (p : PNCounter R) : Int :=\n  (GCounter.value p.1 : Int) - (GCounter.value p.2 : Int)\n\ntheorem incr_inflationary (i : Fin R) : Order.Inflationary (incr (R := R) i) :=\n  fun p => ⟨GCounter.increment_inflationary i p.1, le_refl p.2⟩\n\n/-- Even a decrement moves the *state* upward. -/\ntheorem decr_inflationary (i : Fin R) : Order.Inflationary (decr (R := R) i) :=\n  fun p => ⟨le_refl p.1, GCounter.increment_inflationary i p.2⟩\n\n/-- A decrement lowers the value while the state strictly grows. -/\ntheorem value_decr (i : Fin R) (p : PNCounter R) :\n    value (decr i p) = value p - 1 := by\n  show (GCounter.value p.1 : Int) - (GCounter.value (GCounter.increment i p.2) : Int)\n      = (GCounter.value p.1 : Int) - (GCounter.value p.2 : Int) - 1\n  rw [GCounter.value_increment]\n  omega\n\n/-- Refuted: \"the CRDT's value is monotone\".\n    States only grow; reports may fall. The lattice disciplines what is\n    *remembered*, not what is *reported*. -/\ntheorem value_not_monotone :\n    ¬ (∀ (p q : PNCounter 1), p ⊑ q → value p ≤ value q) := by\n  intro h\n  have hle : (⊥ : PNCounter 1) ⊑ (⊥, GCounter.increment 0 ⊥) :=\n    ⟨bot_le _, bot_le _⟩\n  exact absurd (h _ _ hle) (by decide)\n\n/-- Refuted: the value of a merge is not the max (nor min, nor either\n    argument) of the values — value is a *projection*, not a lattice map. -/\ntheorem value_merge_ne_max :\n    let p : PNCounter 1 := incr 0 ⊥\n    let q : PNCounter 1 := decr 0 ⊥\n    value (p ⊔ q) ≠ max (value p) (value q) := by decide\n\ntheorem value_bot : value (⊥ : PNCounter R) = 0 := by\n  show (GCounter.value (⊥ : GCounter R) : Int) - (GCounter.value (⊥ : GCounter R) : Int) = 0\n  rw [GCounter.value_bot]\n  decide\n\n/-- Interlude: concurrent increments and decrements at two replicas,\n    merged both ways. -/\ndef interlude : List (String × Int) :=\n  let atA := incr 0 (incr 0 (⊥ : PNCounter 2))  -- A: +2\n  let atB := decr 1 (incr 1 (⊥ : PNCounter 2))  -- B: +1 then −1\n  [ (\"A alone\", value atA),\n    (\"B alone\", value atB),\n    (\"A ⊔ B\", value (atA ⊔ atB)),\n    (\"B ⊔ A\", value (atB ⊔ atA)),\n    (\"(A ⊔ B) ⊔ B\", value ((atA ⊔ atB) ⊔ atB)) ]\n\n#eval interlude\n\nend PNCounter",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A value that clamps at zero hides the negative truth: a seed for the last lesson.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def clampedValue {R : Nat} (p : PNCounter R) : Nat :=\n  (PNCounter.value p).toNat\n\nexample : clampedValue (PNCounter.decr 0 (⊥ : PNCounter 1)) = 0 := by decide\nexample : PNCounter.value (PNCounter.decr 0 (⊥ : PNCounter 1)) < 0 := by decide",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "le(PN, (2, 0), (1, 1))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "For one replica, is the state $(2, 0)$ below $(1, 1)$? Answer `true` or `false`.",
      "hints": [
        "Compare componentwise."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "join(product(G, G), ((2, 0), (1, 0)), ((1, 1), (0, 1)))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Two replicas $a$, $b$; write a PN state as $(P, N)$, each half a pair of slots ($a$'s, $b$'s). Replica $a$ holds $((2, 0), (1, 0))$ and $b$ holds $((1, 1), (0, 1))$. Merge them. (The merged state reads $3 - 2 = 1$.)",
      "hints": [
        "$P$ and $N$ merge separately, each slot by the maximum."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A decrement that lowers the state is undone by the next merge with a replica that has not heard of it. Counting decrements as events of their own keeps every update going up: the state is a pair of grow-only counters, and only the value, $P - N$, falls. Sets come next. Adding an element is easy; what is the decrement of a set?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
